<font face="Arial" size="+1"><B>Ultima Online 2 - CoNB Log for 4/27/2000</B></font>
		<p></p>

<font face=arial size=-1 color=black>
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Greetings and salutations.
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: We're going to let the team introduce themselvs and then start with the questions
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: I'm Ubiq, and I'm the lead designer of this desperate band of rebels.
<br><b>(Cynthe)</b>: Hi everyone! I'm Cynthe, community person. It's so great to see you all here! :)
<br><b>(KnightUO2)</b>: Welcome! I'm Knight, the executive producer of UO2
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: I'm Windfeather, one of the desperate rebels. I design sissy roleplayer stuff for UO2.
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: Ahriman here, the Minister of Fiction for UO2.
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: Hiya, I'm Mobius, the Lead Designer of UO2 Live and hardcore number-cruncher.
<br><b>(Cynthe)</b>: Don't let him sell himself short. He IS a sissy roleplayer. :)
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: Bah.
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: I second that.
<br>* Windfeather pulls out the Beast.
<br><b>(BEAST_OSI)</b>: Greetings everyone. Some may recognize me from my work with UO. I'm just a system/world building design monkey extraordinaire.
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: Please remember that this chat is to introduce everyone to the team, they will not discuss development issues at this time, so please keep the questions geared away from them
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: What is everyone's background? Education, prior industry experience, etc...
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Good question. I went to college, where I got a degree in advertising that turned out to be completely useless.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: Lessee...I have a degree in Political Science, so I am mostly involved in the more social aspects of UO2 design. I have a varied background of design, customer service, marketing...all with text MUDs.
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Turns out that the time I was wasting on making and playing MUDs turned out to be where I'd earn up making a living. I ended up on Meridian 59's design staff, and eventually clawed my way to the top.
<br><b>(Cynthe)</b>: Okay, so I'm not on the dev team, but I'll jump in and answer this too... I'm an English major/German minor from University of Texas, and I worked on Ultima Ascension before moving over into community. I get paid to talk... that rocks. :)
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: A couple years later, found myself heeding the summons from Origin to make the best MMPRPG ever. And so here I am.
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: I did my undergraduate work in Biology and Physics. My graduate work in Aerospace Engineering. My previous proects include GemStone III and DragonRealms
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: I did some work on DragonRealms and GemStone, too. W00t!
<br><b>(BEAST_OSI)</b>: I've got an English/Psy degree with a math minor. Been knee deep in UO since the pre-alpha in 96. Taking what I learned in school and UO to UO2 to try and help create a great on-line world.
<br><b>(KnightUO2)</b>: I've been at Origin for about 2 years now. Executive producer for UO2, and even UO for awhile. Worked on UA and other things here. Before that I was Exec Dir at Paramount, overseeing their Interactive group. Before that was COO at a developer, Mission Studios. Before that I was a lawyer (sorry!)
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: Boo.
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: Degree in Literature, History, and Film Production (yes, I spent a lot of time in college). Used to work for OSI back in '89-90 on Ultima 6 and Wing Commander as a tester. Have written mainly screenplays, one of which gained some recognition by virtue of placing in some screenplay competitions.
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: *GearBox* On a personal level, What is the Dev Teams overall opinion of the buying and selling of virtual items for real currency. Do you encourage it, or do you see it ruining gameing the way it is supposed to be?
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: Although Origin policy has been pretty open regarding buying and selling of accounts and items, we are trying to minimize the frequency of this in UO2 through a tight game design.
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: *Avenger* What does the UO2 team think of Linux as a gaming platform, both now and in the future? Will the UO2 client support it?
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Linux is not quite yet a viable platform for a MMPRPG. I would love to support a Linux client, but in a game with a long lifespan that have to be supported forever, additional platforms are costly. Linux fans will have to wait a bit for a Linux UO2 client.
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: *RaK|KoC* What are your plans towards PvPers ? A PvP switch ? The PvP land split a la U:OR ? or even PvP servers ?
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: PVP: We want to keep all kinds of gamers happy in UO2, while still maintaining a few important goals, such as keeping new players safe, allowing people to play unmolested if they so choose, but also having extensive 'wild' areas (with rules) where we can have a more frontier society.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: I've made a couple of posts on this recently, if you want to check out the Stratics boards, and I have seen some links elsewhere to the posts :)
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: Also did some recently on XRGaming, of course <smile>
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: [Amberite] Ahriman, how long will the fiction continue for? It's incredibly well written, and I'd love to see a lot more of it. Will it continue until beta?
<br>* Marduk chuckles
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: There are four parts for each of the three Prequel Stories currently up on the web. The Prequel Stories will conclude after the fourth episode. Additionally, there are plans for a UO2 trilogy of novels to be published by Simon and Schuster. And we intend to continue to support the fiction on the website after launch.
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: Also, the author of our excellent Prequel Fiction is Austen Andrews, a very talented writer I'm sure you'll see more of in the future. I have guided the fiction and provided background for them and I edit the final stories, but I did not write them.
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: Just to set the record straight. :)
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: *deVoca* Question for Ubiq: You mentioned it twice, so is it going in? Many players are already looking forward to juggling flaming kittens
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: I plead the fifth, on the grounds that my answer may or may not get us picketed by the ASPCA.
<br>* Ubiq starts twitching uncontrollably.
<br><b>(KnightUO2)</b>: No lawsuits, please! [joke]
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: *Stimwalt* Don't you think pvp-only servers or pvp-switches will take away from pvp overall? If everyone flocks to safe switches or servers there will be no pvp environment at all
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: PvP servers, switches: The design team is very well aware of the pros and cons of this type of PvP solution...and it is not the one we are using :)
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: [JuSticE] What are your opinions on Macroing (which was very popular in UO)? will it be discouraged in UO2?
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: Alrighty let's see...
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Macroing is bad. Macroing must die. My goal is to design a game where you CAN'T macro anything, and also where it's so fun that you don't WANT to macro anything.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: Hee.
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: opps. :)
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Lofty goals are worth shooting for, I always say.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: I think we're pretty well on our way to meeting that goal.
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: I thought macroing was something you did with sewing needles. Go figure.
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: *Goltar1212* What department is most of the dev teams energy going too at this point in time?(graphics,gameplay, ect. ect.)
<br><b>(KnightUO2)</b>: UO2 is clearly a enormous endeavor. We've got a terrific team focusing on a variety of goals -- all of which are important. Hard to say that one's got more focus than another. For us, we're making sure that the game has got: Cool character customization, stunning world, awesome warfare and great accessibility.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: The Design team (as opposed to programmer team, art team, scripting team, worldbuilding team) is balancing skills for the next few weeks.
<br><b>(KnightUO2)</b>: But if I had to choose one, I think it would be gameplay for emphasis and tech for sheer numbers
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: I'm hoping that by the end of all of this balance, I'll turn Windfeather into a real number crunching designer. :)
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: Grief designer.
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: [Taraturg] Will UO2's story in any way jump out at the player? forcing them to involve themselves within it? if so how... if not fau!
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Now, now, you two. Do I have to turn this thing around?
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: It is indeed our goal (and my burning desire) to have a dynamic story after launch that will be guided by the actions of the players. There will be political intrigues aplenty, several major characters that the players can interact with.
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: And the land will be in a state of turmoil. Conflict is drama. Without it, the story is not very satisfying.
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: We will have events, both epic and more personal.
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: And it's our hope that the players will leap right into the intrigues and choose sides.
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: They'll determine how the various political factions will fare in over the course of time.
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: [Kalar] Looking in any phone book and you will see many; Smiths, Johnson's, Anderson's, and many more common names... So why impose unique names on us?
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Because UO2 is a service and a community. And both of those things suffer when players can hide behind a shield of anonymity.
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Anonymity is the enemy of an online community support team. Because players demand better support and greater accountability, we must take strides to give players and tools ways to identify troublemakers and antisocial elements positively.
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: [RaK|KoC] What will be the consequences for death on UO2 ? No consequences like on UO, a vitae loss a la AC, a stat loss a la EQ ?
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: Death: We will have death.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: On a more serious note...
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: I insist.
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: Plus, have you guys read Neil Gaiman? Death is cute. I insist on her too.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: Death won't be annoying. It will be something to be avoided because it comes with penalties, but it will be something you can prepare for if you're savvy.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: There will not be Permanent Death in UO2!
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: Or any game I ever work for.
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: What do you think of fan sites? How do you feel about people posting both the good and bad about your game? Do you feel that fan sites add to the game or take away from it?
<br><b>(KnightUO2)</b>: We welcome all kinds of feedback. Sometimes it can be good and sometimes it's bad. Even when it is bad, it can be constructive for us. It helps us rethink our plans. Believe it or not, sometimes we are wrong. (Even us!) As for the value that Fan sites add, they are critical. They add enormous value to the community and act as a conduit for information. We couldn't build a great UO2 without them!
<br><b>(Cynthe)</b>: Fansites are really amazing. I work with quite a few of them, and I'm daily amazed at the dedication of the community. It sounds cheesy, I know, but how else can you feel when people put so much time into supporting something that you're doing?
<br><b>(Cynthe)</b>: Of course, not all the sites post stuff that's all rosy, and that's okay too. I see there being a difference between sites that post criticisms of a game because they honestly have concerns, and sites that truly like to cause dissent by posting things like exploitable bugs that will dirupt the game when used.
<br><b>(Cynthe)</b>: It's a fine line, but being such a public game, we nee to expect lots of attention.... and it's really a great thing. :)
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: The fansites so far have been tremendous in allowing us to improve our designs. In particular, the QotWs have been FANTASTIC, and I urge people to keep responding to them.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: Some of the more clever sites are rant sites :)
(Marduk): Thank you for playing :)
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: That wasn't an example of clever.
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: I wish I was cool like that guy.
<br><b>(Cynthe)</b>: *cough* Lum, which I visit 15 times a day... I just can't help myself. I'm a glutton for punishment. :)
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: I love fan sites. The fact that someone cares enough to dedicate the time it takes to run one of those (whether they say good things or bad) is appreciated. Good work, fan site folks!
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: [Amberite] Will it be possible to completely master Magic or Sword fighting in UO2 like it was in UO?
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: I'm very pleased at the amount of depth we've added to our skill sets...
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: I've taken it as a personal quest to insure that even if you decide to be an all out 100% warror...
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: mage, blacksmith, etc...
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: You can still be quite a bit different from another player who is also a 100% warrior, mage, etc...
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: Some of our larger skill systems (like combat and magic), it should be really really hard....
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: if not impossible to learn everything there is to learn.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: He's not kidding. He's killing me with balancing this stuff.
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: You know you love it.
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: *Whol* Do you feel female players are being alienated because popular games represent the female characters in the world in revealing clothing and with well endowed features?
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: Flaming Kitten Blacksmithing should be removed.
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Shhhh! We weren't going to talk about the seeeekret skills.
<br><b>(Cynthe)</b>: I don't know if I can speak for all women, because I'm not what I'd call "typical"... well heck, what's typical?
<br><b>(Cynthe)</b>: Anyway, I like being able to play a character that's not me in real life. I like being an Amazon goddess/warrior. :)
<br><b>(Cynthe)</b>: Those features don't bother me a bit. However, I do agree that you shouldn't be forced to play that look, and I know a lot of women don't want to be forced into bustiers and corsets. I'm in agreement with that, and from what I've seen of UO2 when I sneak into the dev teams' offices, there are alternatives.
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Female players will have options. They will be able to choose between demure, pure, hardened and sexy, among many other choices.
<br>* Lum_ feels the power of his fully armed and operational battlestation
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: We understand that 50% of the people out there are women.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: A couple of us even met one, once :)
<br>* GearBox reminds Lum that this technological terror he has constructed is insignificant nest to the power of a good fansite
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: *EolirinXianve* I don't know if this has been asked already, but how important do you guys feel previous Ultima fiction is?
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: One of my goals, aside from marrying Tyra Banks that is, is to take the previous Ultima fiction and weave it into a seamless (or as seamless as I can make it) tapestry for UO2. The Prequel Fiction is already doing this (you guys are in for some real treats with the next episodes), and I have no intention of stopping.
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: Old Ultima fiction is *very* important to us.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: I second that...we have some big Ultima fans on the team, and we are doing our darnedest to continue the tradition.
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: *Goltar1212* Once UO2 is out, do you think that people will still play UO? If so why?
<br><b>(KnightUO2)</b>: Absolutely! I will still play UO after UO2 ships. UO and UO2 share a lot, but they also are very different from each other. Style, viewpoint, fiction, approach and more will set the two games apart.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: I still play my Thief on Napa, won't see any reason to quit :)
<br><b>(KnightUO2)</b>: We are continuing to add more content to UO. That's a vibrant world on its own. UO2 will be something else for people.
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: Do you find the future player's visions and ideas about the game interesting? Will, the dev boards actually help you make the game, or perhaps it will delay it, since there are infact tons of great well-thought out ideas.
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Most of the design for Ultima Online is 'done light'. We have a good idea of what it is and where we want it to go. That being said, the fans have been instrumental in helping us to tighten the design, and include things that we had not thought of before.
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: The questions of the week, in particular, have been amazing at helping us tighten key parts of our design. Combat and monster AI in particular profited from the fan's feedback.
<br><b>(Lum_)</b>: and the bananas don't hurt
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: There are a TON of great ideas out there (I by no means claim to have a monopoly on them!), and we are eager to get as many of them as we can into the game. Needless to say, though, we must hit beta and we must ship.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: Heh.
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Fortunately, this product has a long life ahead of it AFTER ship, and will have a fully staffed live team. I have no doubt that many of the things that can't make the initial release will eventually find a home in UO2 after launch, thanks to Mobius and the rest of the live team.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: We've planned some things far, far in advance.
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Moral of the story: POST TO THE QUESTION OF THE WEEK!
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: He's not kidding, folks, we take those posts very seriously. Well, the serious ones, anyway.
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: *|Dan|* Are you going to be attending this years E3, and what can we be expecting to see if anything? What games are you interested in seeing at this years E3?
<br><b>(KnightUO2)</b>: E3 will be the "coming out party" for UO2. We've all seen the E3 demo here in Austin, and it is just amazing. We're much farther along than even I thought we'd be. Unfortunately, we will be showing it behind closed doors (sorry guys). Why? Mostly, because we are senstive to tipping our hand to competitors. You'll see the screen shots in PC Gamer (and more coming soon) and they are impressive.
<br><b>(KnightUO2)</b>: The good news is that we're meeting with many of the Fan Sites there to let them see the demo
<br><b>(KnightUO2)</b>: one more thing
<br><b>(KnightUO2)</b>: The PC Gamer cover-mount CD should contain a UO2 trailer of sorts. Worth checking out
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: 'Worth checking out'?
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: The man doesn't do it service :)
<br><b>(KnightUO2)</b>: Yea, but "bad ass" sounds too silly
<br><b>(KnightUO2)</b>: In a public chat
<br>* KnightUO2 Oops
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: "bad ass" doesn't do it service. It's much cooler than that.
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: [Taraturg] How does the team plan to treat warriors in UO2, when magic classes have got most of the eye candy in the past?
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: I've always been partial to playing pure warriors myself in RPGS....
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: and I, too, get frustrated when a high level warrior just gets to contine to swing his sword...
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: but the mages get all of this spiffy stuff...
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: So I don't see why the mages should have all of the fun...
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: I'm working on a combat system that will give warriors some truly spectacular abilities of their own...
<br><b>(Mobius)</b>: with any luck, even the mages will be jealous. ;)
<br><br><b>(Aureus)</b>: *Stimwalt* If one was an aspiring video game developer heading to college soon, are there any courses you could recommend taking? Most Comp Sci courses are geared towards business software, not entertainment...
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: What your mother doesn't want me to tell you: college doesn't count for much when I look at resumes. Most people get into the games industry because (a) they know someone and (b) they can demonstrate real talent.
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Take the time to build a portfolio. Build some Unreal levels. Code on a MUD. Write a lot of stories.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: He's right. Our producer was a theatre major, I believe. I was Political Science, which is good for designing certain aspects of an online RPG.
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: And as for using college to get in, the best way to use college is to learn good coding practices, and to get some kind of, any kind of internship, which will get your foot in the door.
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: As I mentioned before, I was an ad major. I'm here now because I also happened to be an Immortal on the text MUD that Designer Dragon was a player on. =)
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: Working Quality Assurance or Customer Service is a common, and good, way to work up to a designer position, also.
<br>* Vex grins
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: One of our art leads got his start in the business when he was 'discovered' by his prior company selling software at Babbage's...and he's someone whose name you will hear for years to come, he's that good.
<br><b>(KnightUO2)</b>: We have a guy who used to bake cakes for a living too
<br><br><b>(Marduk)</b>: *Cynedrin* Since you are keeping UO up when UO2 ships, what are you going to do about the inconsistancies in storyline, since one is in the past and one in the future? Or, if the storyline will remain consistant, what will you do about people learning storyline in UO in advance by perusing UO2?
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: UO2's chronology will break away from UO's when the Cataclysm occurs. Since there are several shards, each containing their own complete version of Sosaria, only a few of those shards will splinter and be affected by the Cataclysm. The UO shards will continue, setting their own destinies. The UO2 shards will become an alternate timeline, creating the 200 year history that will lead up to the launch of UO2. But the birth of the world is very much
<br><br><b>(Myschyf)</b>: [Taraturg] Will artificial intelligence in UO2 be at least slightly intelligent? I haven't seen anything worth getting excited over in past MMORPGs. Any chance for treats like flocks/ attack groups(slightly organized)/monster communities etc...
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Yes.
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Okay, okay, more info
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Yes, monsters will work together, yes they will attempt to perform tactics, yes, groups of monsters should act as worthy foes for groups of players.
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Yes, this is ambitious, yes we think it will work. We fortunately have some code from Bullfrog that we could borrow that helped us along quite a bit.
<br><br><b>(Marduk)</b>: btw, to finish Ubiq's statement: ' the birth of the world is very much rooted in the history of UO.'
<br><b>(Marduk)</b>: err Ahriman's
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: Thanks, I'm much better looking.
<br>* Ubiq whaps Marduk. Don't confuse me with Ahriman.
<br>* Marduk cowers
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: that's just rude =p
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: Yeah, what he said. ;)
<br>* Ubiq grins.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: You're not better looking. You're just hairier.
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: You're just jealous cuz you can't grow a beard.
<br><b>(Lum_)</b>: So my one and only question
<br><b>(Lum_)</b>: Is Lady MOI going to play UO2?
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: She told me she would.
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Will you like us more if I say yes? =)
<br><b>(Lum_)</b>: Only if she's in the PvP enabled zone =)
<br><br><b>(Marduk)</b>: Alright folks
<br><b>(Marduk)</b>: That concludes our chat for tonight
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: I'm sure she'll play...I wish I could see the game for the first time AS a player.
<br>* Vex slaps Lum_ around a bit with a large jellybean
<br><b>(Marduk)</b>: We want to really thank the folks at OSI for putting up with us :)
<br><b>(KnightUO2)</b>: Thanks for joining us!
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: That's the one real drag to making a game. You know everything about it when it opens!
<br><b>(KnightUO2)</b>: Our pleasure. Let's do it again soon.
<br><b>(Cynthe)</b>: It's great to get to talk to you guys a bit, and I hope you'll all stick around as we're able to give you more over the coming months. :)
<br><b>(Ahriman_)</b>: Keep those excellent posts and suggestions coming, folks.
<br><b>(Windfeather)</b>: Keep posting to the message boards - I enjoy reading and responding to what you have to say.
<br><b>(Ubiq)</b>: Always a pleasure, folks. Until next time we meet....
</font>

</td></tr></table>

